ence/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/d0bd5d7fc7f1d7b487fd940af95b5abb7e2fb134c02a55dbf9eb7d191abccca8.png)\n\nNow, since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/c93c5bf19eefeb4da980b81d46c4df10b3ff10aca21937aed885ef4111b1c9a7.png) lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/f49e0503f6588c93dd1fd274c63f60c06b0eb77d5dce2d12dba750107139f124.png) as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/96e40550e19a81ffb655cd359242d8b0a37b1dde12e442252f976397a09a9760.png)\n\nto three decimal places.\n\nFinally, substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/5dcf5f8d9a0eb6d36f76152105bcb5a9af61a34bc99719183df2044d7f113edb.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/11dd78699cf02b8b10f1bd6dc78ee987d26954fc8b51a2e8327c2f24b5552458.png) into ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/9ea3d5e030449e3b1da133b0aa004c6d26702021bf66e5672abe2bdd2c69eb9b.png) we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/dd0d831b250bbf985783cbb745a23c709a481ba08018395c683c3e2d4b00284a.png)" 13194139536109 true] [17592186087475 1145 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= x + \\textrm{i}y \\\\\n&=-3 + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form\n\n$$\nz = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nr= \\mid z \\mid\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arg(z)\n$$\n\nwe first calculate $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg (z) &= π - \\mid \\arctan (\\frac{y}{x}) \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\mid \\arctan (\\frac{2}{3}) \\mid \\\\\n&= π - 0.588 \\\\\n&= 2.554\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places.\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{13}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta=2.554\n$$\n\ninto\n\n$$\nz=re^{\\textrm{i} \\theta}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nz = \\sqrt{13}\\,\\textrm{e}^{2.554\\,\\textrm{i}}\\,\n$$" 13194139536109 false] [17592186087476 1145 "To write our complex number in the form\n\n$$\nz = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nr= \\mid z \\mid\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arg(z)\n$$\n\nwe first calculate $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ = \\arg (z) &= \\arctan (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\arctan (1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nr=4\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ninto\n\n$$\nz=re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nz = 4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{e}^{\\pi\\textrm{i}/4}\n$$" 13194139536109 false] [17592186087476 1145 "To write our complex number in the form $z = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta = \\arg(z),$ we first calculate $r\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into $z=re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ we get\n\n$$\nz = 4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{e}^{\\pi\\textrm{i}/4}.\n$$" 13194139536109 true] [17592186087671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384031 13194139536109 true] [17592186087671 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384045 13194139536109 true] [17592186087672 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384038 13194139536109 true] [17592186087998 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536109 true] [17592186087998 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/d7eb4042a5422d631c867cba1c1fa9c52b400d0061fd5fe7d2fa380d721dbac5.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." 13194139536109 true] [17592186087998 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/898/Source/Images/q-17517.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}θ}$, giving $θ$ in radians to three decimal places." 13194139536109 false] [17592186087998 1145 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =4+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the first quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ≈0.644.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=5$ and $\\theta =0.644$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.644\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" 13194139536109 true] [17592186088231 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/c5d36b72e7e2ff6754152a559d64bf3610708c9a2d3790c859bc9abacc9220b6.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." 13194139536109 true] [17592186088231 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059847/assets/c5d36b72e7e2ff6754152a559d64bf3610708c9a2d3790c859bc9abacc9220b6.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." 13194139536109 false] [17592186088232 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536109 true] [17592186088232 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nWrite the complex number $z =-1 + \\textrm{i}$ in exponential form." 13194139536109 false] [17592186088232 1100 "Write the complex number $z=-1+\\text{i}$ in exponential form." 13194139536109 true] [17592186088232 1145 "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$" 13194139536109 true] [17592186277519 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/ba77669a8e0ffd3e69bfc8e5322d7042c318a55b4cd486ac1fac591d3b016e43.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to the nearest tenth." 13194139536109 true] [17592186277519 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973361/assets/ba77669a8e0ffd3e69bfc8e5322d7042c318a55b4cd486ac1fac591d3b016e43.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to the nearest tenth." 13194139536109 false] [17592186384031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384032 13194139536109 true] [17592186384031 1089 "q-71977" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536109 true] [17592186384031 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077408/assets/a02f84856db56412060d6f376f3d926e3fb05581ee36ef9b5e4d2cc570e8438d.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." 13194139536109 true] [17592186384031 1113 #uuid "0c35421b-6fda-58ba-a484-a63392e2ff00" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384031 1145 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384033 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384034 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384035 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384036 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384037 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1041 "selection" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384034 13194139536109 true] [17592186384032 1110 #uuid "3b33d743-b84e-5a1c-b068-3d2785829e58" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384033 1034 "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536109 true] [17592186384033 1111 #uuid "c51b3536-c284-5d28-8adc-ff6837d788bb" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384034 1034 "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536109 true] [17592186384034 1111 #uuid "bd6e07f2-a1a8-5c1a-aaf0-4be796cdd52b" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384035 1034 "z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536109 true] [17592186384035 1111 #uuid "1b0da3ec-a3b7-5fc1-be5b-c509ba4a9f89" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384036 1034 "z=3\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536109 true] [17592186384036 1111 #uuid "510f1f24-da71-5fb0-8b1e-55993f709c0b" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384037 1034 "z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536109 true] [17592186384037 1111 #uuid "1e6b2906-4026-5c71-828f-542888675ef7" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384038 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384039 13194139536109 true] [17592186384038 1089 "q-71982" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384038 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536109 true] [17592186384038 1100 "Write the complex number $z=-2+2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ in exponential form." 13194139536109 true] [17592186384038 1113 #uuid "1956a1cf-0621-5528-be75-0d024fb52de7" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384038 1145 "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384040 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384041 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384042 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384043 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384044 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1041 "selection" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384044 13194139536109 true] [17592186384039 1110 #uuid "f3f5060a-1c15-5222-8845-c82dc040cd70" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384040 1034 "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" 13194139536109 true] [17592186384040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536109 true] [17592186384040 1111 #uuid "cb